IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `clatas-dates`
IMPORT `clatas-common`

-- Civil Liability Act 2002 (Tas) -- Part 10C (child abuse: liability of organisations) and s 7AA (an organisation's
-- apology for child abuse). Asked from the seat of the person abused as a child who sues, or may sue, the organisation.
--
-- s 3B(1A) keeps Parts 4 and 10C for "an intentional act that is child abuse, within the meaning of section 49J(3)".
-- s 4(7), (8): ss 49H and 49J apply "only in respect of child abuse perpetrated after the commencement of that section"
-- (1 May 2020, from the history note "Applied:01 May 2020"; abuse on that day is read as after it, fork F2). s 4(9):
-- Division 4 "extends to child abuse proceedings in respect of child abuse perpetrated before the commencement of that
-- Division". s 49E: an organisation and its successor "are, for the purposes of this Part, taken to be the same
-- organisation". s 49C: "organisation means any organisation, whether incorporated or not, and includes a public sector
-- body but does not include the State"; "child means a person who has not attained the age of 18 years".
--
-- The one duty with a deadline the claimant meets, s 49P(4) (the organisation must identify its associated trusts within
-- 28 days after the plaintiff's application), is a regulative rule run by EVALTRACE inside a question that takes plain
-- facts. No exported question takes a DEONTIC or an EVENT.
--
-- Copyright (c) State of Tasmania. Quoted from an unofficial plain-text copy under CC BY 4.0 (SOURCE-LICENSE.md).
-- Based on material from the Tasmanian Legislation website at 6 November 2026.
-- BASELINE ENCODING. Not for public use as advice. See NOTES.md.

§ `Part 10C Division 1 -- What child abuse is (ss 49C, 49H(5), 49J(3), 49L)`

-- ss 49H(5), 49J(3), 49L: "child abuse, in relation to a child, means -- (a) sexual abuse, or physical abuse, of the
-- child; and (b) any psychological abuse of the child that arises from the sexual abuse or physical abuse -- but does
-- not include an act that is lawful at the time at which it occurs." "Sexual abuse", "physical abuse" and "psychological
-- abuse" are not defined: asked as facts.
DECLARE `What was done to me` IS ONE OF
    `sexual abuse`
    `physical abuse`
    `psychological abuse arising from sexual or physical abuse`
    `psychological abuse alone, or neglect, or something else`

GIVEN w IS A `What was done to me`, `my age then` IS A NUMBER, `it was lawful when it happened` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `it is child abuse (ss 49H(5), 49J(3))` w `my age then` `it was lawful when it happened` IF
        `my age then` LESS THAN 18
    AND (NOT `it was lawful when it happened`)
    AND `the kind is in the definition`
    WHERE
        `the kind is in the definition` MEANS
            CONSIDER w
            WHEN `psychological abuse alone, or neglect, or something else` THEN FALSE
            OTHERWISE TRUE

@export Is what was done to me "child abuse" for the organisational liability rules (Civil Liability Act 2002 (Tas) ss 49C, 49H(5), 49J(3))? Sexual or physical abuse of a person under 18, and psychological abuse arising from it, but not an act that was lawful when it happened.
GIVEN w IS A `What was done to me` @desc What was done to you: sexual abuse, physical abuse, psychological abuse arising from either, or something else.
      `my age then` IS A NUMBER @desc Your age, in whole years, when it happened.
      `it was lawful when it happened` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the act lawful at the time it happened?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Is it child abuse` w `my age then` `it was lawful when it happened` MEANS
    IF `it is child abuse (ss 49H(5), 49J(3))` w `my age then` `it was lawful when it happened`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE

§ `Part 10C Division 2 -- The organisation's duty to prevent child abuse (ss 49F to 49H)`

-- s 49F: "(a) an organisation is responsible for a child if it (including any part of it) exercises care, supervision or
-- authority in respect of the child, or purports to do so or is obliged by law to do so; and (b) if an organisation
-- ... delegates the exercise of care, supervision or authority in respect of the child to another organisation (in whole
-- or in part), each organisation is responsible for the child."
DECLARE `The organisation's place in my care` IS ONE OF
    `it exercised, or purported to exercise, care, supervision or authority over me`
    `it was obliged by law to exercise care, supervision or authority over me`
    `it delegated care, supervision or authority over me to another organisation`
    `none of these`

-- s 49G(1): "an individual associated with an organisation includes, but is not limited to including, an individual who
-- is an office holder, officer, employee, owner, volunteer, or contractor, of the organisation and also includes -- (a)
-- if the organisation is a religious organisation -- a religious leader (such as a priest or minister) or member of the
-- personnel of the organisation; and (b) an individual who is prescribed ...". (2): "not associated with an organisation
-- solely because the organisation wholly or partly funds or regulates another organisation." (3): an individual
-- associated with an organisation to which care was delegated is also associated with the delegating organisation. An
-- open "includes, but is not limited to": an individual outside the list is asked as a fact (fork F6).
DECLARE `The abuser's link to the organisation` IS ONE OF
    `office holder, officer, employee, owner, volunteer or contractor`
    `religious leader or personnel of a religious organisation`
    `associated with an organisation to which it delegated my care`
    `otherwise associated with it`
    `linked only because it funds or regulates the abuser's organisation`
    `no link to the organisation`

GIVEN l IS A `The abuser's link to the organisation`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the abuser is associated with the organisation (s 49G)` l IF
    CONSIDER l
    WHEN `linked only because it funds or regulates the abuser's organisation` THEN FALSE
    WHEN `no link to the organisation` THEN FALSE
    OTHERWISE TRUE

DECLARE `What s 49H says about my claim` IS ONE OF
    `the organisation is presumed to have breached its duty of care (s 49H(3))`
    `no presumption: the organisation has established it took reasonable precautions (s 49H(3))`
    `no presumption: the facts s 49H(3) needs are not established`
    `s 49H does not apply`

-- s 49H(2): "An organisation that has responsibility for a child must take reasonable precautions to prevent an
-- individual associated with the organisation who, by virtue of being associated with the organisation, has -- (a)
-- authority, power or control over the child; or (b) the trust of the child; or (c) the ability to achieve intimacy with
-- the child -- from being able, by virtue of that authority, power, control, trust or ability, to perpetrate child abuse
-- on the child." (3): "In proceedings against an organisation involving a breach of the duty of care imposed by this
-- section, the organisation is presumed to have breached its duty of care if the plaintiff establishes that an
-- individual associated with the organisation -- (a) perpetrated child abuse on a child; and (b) had, by virtue of being
-- associated with the organisation -- (i) authority, power or control over the child; or (ii) the trust of the child; or
-- (iii) the ability to achieve intimacy with the child; and (c) was able, by virtue of that authority, power, control,
-- trust or ability, to perpetrate the child abuse on the child -- unless the organisation establishes that it took
-- reasonable precautions to prevent the child abuse." (1): the duty "forms part of a cause of action in negligence".
@export Is the organisation presumed to have breached its duty to prevent the child abuse I suffered (Civil Liability Act 2002 (Tas) ss 4(7), 49F to 49H)? For abuse after 1 May 2020: if the organisation was responsible for you and you establish that someone associated with it abused you, having (through that association) authority, power or control over you, your trust, or the ability to achieve intimacy with you, and was able to abuse you through it, the organisation is presumed in breach unless it establishes that it took reasonable precautions.
GIVEN `the day the abuse happened` IS A DATE @desc The day the abuse happened (if over a period, the last day you ask about). ISO date.
      w IS A `What was done to me` @desc What was done to you.
      `my age then` IS A NUMBER @desc Your age, in whole years, when it happened.
      `it was lawful when it happened` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the act lawful at the time it happened?
      `the defendant is the State itself` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the defendant the State of Tasmania itself? (Agencies, government businesses, State-owned companies, statutory authorities and councils are organisations; the State itself is not.)
      p IS A `The organisation's place in my care` @desc How the organisation (or any part of it, or an organisation it succeeded) was placed in your care.
      l IS A `The abuser's link to the organisation` @desc How the abuser was linked to the organisation.
      `through that link the abuser had authority, power, control, my trust or the ability to achieve intimacy with me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc By virtue of being associated with the organisation, did the abuser have authority, power or control over you, your trust, or the ability to achieve intimacy with you?
      `the abuser was able to abuse me through that` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the abuser able to abuse you by virtue of that authority, power, control, trust or ability?
      `the organisation establishes it took reasonable precautions` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the organisation established that it took reasonable precautions to prevent the abuse?
GIVETH A `What s 49H says about my claim`
`Is the organisation presumed in breach` `the day the abuse happened` w `my age then` `it was lawful when it happened` `the defendant is the State itself` p l `through that link the abuser had authority, power, control, my trust or the ability to achieve intimacy with me` `the abuser was able to abuse me through that` `the organisation establishes it took reasonable precautions` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `the day the abuse happened` `falls before` `Part 10C commenced`
            THEN `s 49H does not apply`
        IF `the defendant is the State itself`
            THEN `s 49H does not apply`
        IF `the organisation is not responsible for me`
            THEN `s 49H does not apply`
        IF      (NOT (`it is child abuse (ss 49H(5), 49J(3))` w `my age then` `it was lawful when it happened`))
             OR (NOT (`the abuser is associated with the organisation (s 49G)` l))
             OR (NOT `through that link the abuser had authority, power, control, my trust or the ability to achieve intimacy with me`)
             OR (NOT `the abuser was able to abuse me through that`)
            THEN `no presumption: the facts s 49H(3) needs are not established`
        IF `the organisation establishes it took reasonable precautions`
            THEN `no presumption: the organisation has established it took reasonable precautions (s 49H(3))`
        OTHERWISE `the organisation is presumed to have breached its duty of care (s 49H(3))`
    WHERE
        `the organisation is not responsible for me` MEANS
            CONSIDER p
            WHEN `none of these` THEN TRUE
            OTHERWISE FALSE

§ `Part 10C Division 3 -- Vicarious liability (ss 49I, 49J)`

-- s 49I(1): "employee, in relation to an organisation, includes an individual who is akin to an employee of the
-- organisation." (2): "An individual is akin to an employee of an organisation if the individual's role within the
-- organisation -- (a) is for the aims or purposes of the organisation; and (b) gives the individual authority, power or
-- control over a child or enables the individual to achieve intimacy with, or the trust of, a child." (3): "Despite
-- subsection (2), an individual is not akin to an employee if the individual's role within the organisation is carried
-- out for a recognisably independent business of the individual or of another person or organisation." No regulation
-- under (4) is deposited.
GIVEN `the abuser was an employee` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `the role was for the organisation's aims or purposes` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `the role gave authority, power or control over a child, or the trust of or intimacy with a child` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `the role was carried out for a recognisably independent business` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the abuser is an employee for s 49J (s 49I)` `the abuser was an employee` `the role was for the organisation's aims or purposes` `the role gave authority, power or control over a child, or the trust of or intimacy with a child` `the role was carried out for a recognisably independent business` IF
        `the abuser was an employee`
    OR (    `the role was for the organisation's aims or purposes`
        AND `the role gave authority, power or control over a child, or the trust of or intimacy with a child`
        AND (NOT `the role was carried out for a recognisably independent business`))

-- s 49J(1): "An organisation is vicariously liable for child abuse perpetrated against a child by a person who is an
-- employee of the organisation if, at the time at which the abuse was perpetrated -- (a) the person, by virtue of being
-- such an employee, had -- (i) authority, power or control over the child; or (ii) the trust of the child; or (iii) the
-- ability to achieve intimacy with the child; and (b) the person was able, by virtue of that authority, power, control,
-- trust or ability, to perpetrate the child abuse on the child." (2): "This section does not affect, and is in addition
-- to, the common law as it applies with respect to vicarious liability." So where s 49J does not make the organisation
-- liable, the common law still may: NOTHING, not FALSE.
@export Is the organisation vicariously liable for the child abuse its employee, or someone akin to an employee, committed against me (Civil Liability Act 2002 (Tas) ss 4(8), 49I, 49J)? For abuse after 1 May 2020: yes, if through that role the abuser had authority, power or control over you, your trust, or the ability to achieve intimacy with you, and was able to abuse you through it. Someone whose role served the organisation's aims and gave them that position is akin to an employee, unless the role was for a recognisably independent business. Answers true where s 49J makes it liable, nothing where s 49J leaves it to the common law.
GIVEN `the day the abuse happened` IS A DATE @desc The day the abuse happened. ISO date.
      w IS A `What was done to me` @desc What was done to you.
      `my age then` IS A NUMBER @desc Your age, in whole years, when it happened.
      `it was lawful when it happened` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the act lawful at the time it happened?
      `the defendant is the State itself` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the defendant the State of Tasmania itself (rather than an agency, government business, council or other organisation)?
      `the abuser was an employee` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the abuser an employee of the organisation?
      `the role was for the organisation's aims or purposes` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If not an employee: was the abuser's role within the organisation for its aims or purposes?
      `the role gave authority, power or control over a child, or the trust of or intimacy with a child` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did that role give the abuser authority, power or control over a child, or enable them to achieve intimacy with, or the trust of, a child?
      `the role was carried out for a recognisably independent business` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the role carried out for a recognisably independent business of the abuser or of someone else?
      `through the role the abuser had authority, power, control, my trust or the ability to achieve intimacy with me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc At the time, by virtue of being such an employee, did the abuser have authority, power or control over you, your trust, or the ability to achieve intimacy with you?
      `the abuser was able to abuse me through that` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the abuser able to abuse you by virtue of that authority, power, control, trust or ability?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Is the organisation vicariously liable` `the day the abuse happened` w `my age then` `it was lawful when it happened` `the defendant is the State itself` `the abuser was an employee` `the role was for the organisation's aims or purposes` `the role gave authority, power or control over a child, or the trust of or intimacy with a child` `the role was carried out for a recognisably independent business` `through the role the abuser had authority, power, control, my trust or the ability to achieve intimacy with me` `the abuser was able to abuse me through that` MEANS
    IF          (NOT (`the day the abuse happened` `falls before` `Part 10C commenced`))
            AND (NOT `the defendant is the State itself`)
            AND `it is child abuse (ss 49H(5), 49J(3))` w `my age then` `it was lawful when it happened`
            AND `the abuser is an employee for s 49J (s 49I)` `the abuser was an employee` `the role was for the organisation's aims or purposes` `the role gave authority, power or control over a child, or the trust of or intimacy with a child` `the role was carried out for a recognisably independent business`
            AND `through the role the abuser had authority, power, control, my trust or the ability to achieve intimacy with me`
            AND `the abuser was able to abuse me through that`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING

§ `Part 10C Division 4 -- Suing an unincorporated organisation (ss 49L to 49R)`

-- s 49M(1): "Child abuse proceedings may be commenced or continue against an unincorporated organisation in the name of
-- the organisation, or in a name reasonably sufficient to identify the organisation, as if the organisation had legal
-- personality." s 49L: "child abuse proceedings means proceedings for a civil claim arising from child abuse, whether the
-- claim arises under this Part or the common law". s 49N(3): if the unincorporated organisation is a Government
-- department established under s 11 of the State Service Act 2000, "the State is taken to be appointed as the proper
-- defendant". s 4(9): Division 4 reaches abuse before it commenced.
DECLARE `Whom I sue` IS ONE OF
    `I can sue the unincorporated organisation in its own name, or a name reasonably sufficient to identify it (s 49M(1))`
    `the State is taken to be the proper defendant for the Government department (s 49N(3))`
    `Division 4 is not needed: the organisation is incorporated`
    `Division 4 does not apply: my claim is not for child abuse`

@export Can I sue an unincorporated organisation (such as a church, club or association that is not a company) for child abuse in its own name (Civil Liability Act 2002 (Tas) ss 4(9), 49L to 49N)? Yes: child abuse proceedings may be started or continued against it in its name, or a name reasonably sufficient to identify it, as if it were a legal person, whenever the abuse happened; for a Government department the State is taken to be the proper defendant.
GIVEN w IS A `What was done to me` @desc What was done to you.
      `my age then` IS A NUMBER @desc Your age, in whole years, when it happened.
      `it was lawful when it happened` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the act lawful at the time it happened?
      `the organisation is incorporated` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the organisation incorporated (for example a company or incorporated association), so that it can already be sued?
      `it is a Government department` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the organisation a Government department established under s 11 of the State Service Act 2000?
GIVETH A `Whom I sue`
`Can I sue the unincorporated organisation` w `my age then` `it was lawful when it happened` `the organisation is incorporated` `it is a Government department` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF NOT (`it is child abuse (ss 49H(5), 49J(3))` w `my age then` `it was lawful when it happened`)
            THEN `Division 4 does not apply: my claim is not for child abuse`
        IF `the organisation is incorporated`
            THEN `Division 4 is not needed: the organisation is incorporated`
        IF `it is a Government department`
            THEN `the State is taken to be the proper defendant for the Government department (s 49N(3))`
        OTHERWISE `I can sue the unincorporated organisation in its own name, or a name reasonably sufficient to identify it (s 49M(1))`

-- s 49P(1)(a): s 49P applies if "child abuse proceedings are commenced against an unincorporated organisation and no
-- suitable proper defendant is appointed as a proper defendant for the organisation by the end of the period of 60 days
-- after the unincorporated organisation (or a management member ...) is served with notice of the commencement of the
-- proceedings". The period ends with the 60th day after service; an application can be made from the next day (fork F4).
GIVEN `the day the organisation was served` IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`the last day of the 60 days (s 49P(1)(a))` `the day the organisation was served` MEANS
    `days after` `the day the organisation was served` 60

@export From what day can I ask the court to appoint the trustees of the organisation's associated trust as the proper defendant, if the organisation has not appointed a suitable one (Civil Liability Act 2002 (Tas) s 49P(1)(a))? From the day after the end of the 60 days after the organisation (or a management member) was served with notice of the proceedings.
GIVEN `the day the organisation was served` IS A DATE @desc The day the unincorporated organisation, or a member of its management, was served with notice that you had started the proceedings. ISO date.
GIVETH A DATE
`From what day can I apply for the trustees to be appointed` `the day the organisation was served` MEANS
    `days after` (`the last day of the 60 days (s 49P(1)(a))` `the day the organisation was served`) 1

-- s 49P(3): "A trust is an associated trust of an unincorporated organisation ... if one or more of the following apply:
-- (a) the organisation has, either directly or indirectly, the power to control the application of the income, or the
-- distribution of the property, of the trust; (b) the organisation has the power to obtain the beneficial enjoyment of
-- the property, or income, of the trust ...; (c) ... the power to appoint or remove the trustee or trustees ...; (d) ...
-- the power to appoint or remove beneficiaries ...; (e) the trustee or trustees ... are accustomed or under an
-- obligation, whether formal or informal, to act according to the directions, instructions or wishes of the
-- organisation; (f) ... the power to determine the outcome of any other decisions about the trust's operations; (g) a
-- member of the organisation, or a management member ..., has, under the trust deed ..., a power of a kind referred to
-- in paragraph (a), (b), (c), (d), (e) or (f), but only if the trust has been established or used for the activities of
-- the organisation or for the benefit of the organisation."
DECLARE `The organisation's link to the trust` IS ONE OF
    `it controls the application of the trust's income or the distribution of its property`
    `it can obtain the beneficial enjoyment of the trust's property or income`
    `it can appoint or remove the trustees`
    `it can appoint or remove beneficiaries`
    `the trustees are accustomed or obliged to act on its directions or wishes`
    `it can determine the outcome of other decisions about the trust's operations`
    `a member or management member has such a power under the trust deed, and the trust was established or used for the organisation`
    `a member or management member has such a power under the trust deed, but the trust was not established or used for the organisation`
    `the trust was formerly associated, and the court considers it ceased to be so to avoid liability and that not appointing its trustees would be unjust`
    `no link to the trust`

GIVEN t IS A `The organisation's link to the trust`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the trustees can be considered (s 49P(2), (3))` t IF
    CONSIDER t
    WHEN `a member or management member has such a power under the trust deed, but the trust was not established or used for the organisation` THEN FALSE
    WHEN `no link to the trust` THEN FALSE
    OTHERWISE TRUE

DECLARE `Appointing the trustees` IS ONE OF
    `the court can appoint the trustees as the proper defendant on my application (s 49P(2))`
    `not yet: the 60 days after service have not ended (s 49P(1)(a))`
    `no: a suitable proper defendant has been appointed (s 49P(1))`
    `no: the trust is not an associated trust of the organisation (s 49P(2), (3))`
    `no: the trustees are not a suitable proper defendant (ss 49O, 49P(2))`

-- s 49P(1)(b): it also applies if, "after the end of that period, the proper defendant that is appointed ceases to be a
-- suitable proper defendant". s 49O: "an entity is suitable to be appointed as a proper defendant for an organisation if
-- -- (a) the entity is able to be sued in this State; and (b) the entity, or, if the entity is a trustee of a trust, the
-- trust, has sufficient assets in Australia to satisfy any judgment or order that may arise out of child abuse
-- proceedings against the unincorporated organisation." s 49P(2): "The court ... may, on the application of the
-- plaintiff, appoint the trustees of one or more of the following trusts to be a proper defendant of an organisation if
-- the trustees are a suitable proper defendant for the organisation: (a) an associated trust of the organisation; (b) a
-- trust that was formerly an associated trust ..., if the court considers that -- (i) the trust ceased to be an
-- associated trust in an attempt to avoid trust property being applied to satisfy any liability that may be incurred
-- in child abuse proceedings; and (ii) it would be unjust not to appoint the trustees of the trust."
@export Can the court appoint the trustees of a trust linked to the unincorporated organisation as the proper defendant to my child abuse claim (Civil Liability Act 2002 (Tas) ss 49O, 49P)? Once 60 days after service have passed with no suitable proper defendant appointed (or the one appointed stops being suitable), the court may on your application appoint the trustees of an associated trust (or of a former one moved out of reach to avoid liability) if they can be sued in Tasmania and the trust has enough assets in Australia to meet a judgment.
GIVEN `the day the organisation was served` IS A DATE @desc The day the unincorporated organisation, or a member of its management, was served with notice of your proceedings. ISO date.
      `the day asked about` IS A DATE @desc The day you are asking about (usually today, or the day you would apply). ISO date.
      `a suitable proper defendant was appointed within the 60 days and still is suitable` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did the organisation appoint a proper defendant, able to be sued in Tasmania with enough assets in Australia, by the end of the 60 days, and is it still suitable?
      t IS A `The organisation's link to the trust` @desc How the organisation is linked to the trust (if more than one applies, any one).
      `the trustees can be sued in Tasmania` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Can the trustees be sued in Tasmania?
      `the trust has enough assets in Australia to meet a judgment` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the trust have sufficient assets in Australia to satisfy any judgment or order that may arise from your proceedings?
GIVETH A `Appointing the trustees`
`Can the court appoint the trustees as proper defendant` `the day the organisation was served` `the day asked about` `a suitable proper defendant was appointed within the 60 days and still is suitable` t `the trustees can be sued in Tasmania` `the trust has enough assets in Australia to meet a judgment` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF NOT (`the day asked about` `falls after` (`the last day of the 60 days (s 49P(1)(a))` `the day the organisation was served`))
            THEN `not yet: the 60 days after service have not ended (s 49P(1)(a))`
        IF `a suitable proper defendant was appointed within the 60 days and still is suitable`
            THEN `no: a suitable proper defendant has been appointed (s 49P(1))`
        IF NOT (`the trustees can be considered (s 49P(2), (3))` t)
            THEN `no: the trust is not an associated trust of the organisation (s 49P(2), (3))`
        IF NOT (`the trustees can be sued in Tasmania` AND `the trust has enough assets in Australia to meet a judgment`)
            THEN `no: the trustees are not a suitable proper defendant (ss 49O, 49P(2))`
        OTHERWISE `the court can appoint the trustees as the proper defendant on my application (s 49P(2))`

-- s 49P(4): "Within 28 days after the making of an application by a plaintiff under this section, the unincorporated
-- organisation must identify to the court any associated trusts of the organisation, including by identifying the
-- financial capacity of those trusts." A regulative rule on the organisation, counted in days from the application.
DECLARE `Trust party` IS ONE OF `the unincorporated organisation`
DECLARE `Trust action` IS ONE OF `identify its associated trusts and their financial capacity to the court (s 49P(4))`

GIVETH A DEONTIC `Trust party` `Trust action`
`s 49P(4) -- the organisation must identify its associated trusts` MEANS
    PARTY `the unincorporated organisation`
    MUST `identify its associated trusts and their financial capacity to the court (s 49P(4))`
    WITHIN 28
    HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST BREACH BY `the unincorporated organisation` BECAUSE "s 49P(4): associated trusts not identified to the court within 28 days after the plaintiff's application"

@export By what day must the unincorporated organisation identify its associated trusts, and their financial capacity, to the court after I apply for the trustees to be appointed (Civil Liability Act 2002 (Tas) s 49P(4))? Within 28 days after your application.
GIVEN `the day I applied` IS A DATE @desc The day you made your application to the court under s 49P. ISO date.
GIVETH A DATE
`By what day must the organisation identify its trusts` `the day I applied` MEANS
    `days after` `the day I applied` 28

DECLARE `The organisation's trusts` IS ONE OF
    `the duty is kept, or can still be kept (s 49P(4))`
    `late: the organisation did not identify its associated trusts within 28 days after my application (s 49P(4))`

-- Runs the duty over what happened. If the organisation has identified its trusts, the event is on that day; if not, an
-- identification on `the day asked about` stands in for "had it done so that day": if that would not keep the duty, it
-- is late. On the stable l4 a CONSIDER on an EVALTRACE matches FULFILLED only.
@export Is the unincorporated organisation late in identifying its associated trusts to the court (Civil Liability Act 2002 (Tas) s 49P(4))? It must identify them, with their financial capacity, within 28 days after your application under s 49P.
GIVEN `the day I applied` IS A DATE @desc The day you made your application under s 49P. ISO date.
      `it has identified its trusts` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the organisation identified its associated trusts (and their financial capacity) to the court?
      `the day it identified them` IS A DATE @desc If so, the day it did. Otherwise any date; it is ignored.
      `the day asked about` IS A DATE @desc The day you are asking about (usually today). ISO date.
GIVETH A `The organisation's trusts`
`Is the organisation late identifying its trusts` `the day I applied` `it has identified its trusts` `the day it identified them` `the day asked about` MEANS
    CONSIDER EVALTRACE `s 49P(4) -- the organisation must identify its associated trusts` 0 `the events`
    WHEN FULFILLED THEN `the duty is kept, or can still be kept (s 49P(4))`
    OTHERWISE `late: the organisation did not identify its associated trusts within 28 days after my application (s 49P(4))`
    WHERE
        `the day of identifying` MEANS IF `it has identified its trusts` THEN `the day it identified them` ELSE `the day asked about`
        `the events` MEANS LIST (EVENT `the unincorporated organisation` `identify its associated trusts and their financial capacity to the court (s 49P(4))` (`the days from` `the day I applied` `to` `the day of identifying`))

-- s 49R(2): "Any liability of a trustee incurred by the trustee as a proper defendant in child abuse proceedings is
-- limited to the value of the trust property." (6): the liability "includes any unpaid judgment debt arising from the
-- proceedings, any amount paid in settlement of the proceedings and any costs associated with the proceedings." (1)(c),
-- (3): the trustees may apply trust property to it, and are indemnified out of it.
@export How much can I recover from the trustees of the associated trust appointed as the proper defendant (Civil Liability Act 2002 (Tas) s 49R)? Their liability, including the judgment debt, settlement and costs, is limited to the value of the trust property.
GIVEN `the judgment, settlement and costs` IS A NUMBER @desc The total, in dollars, of the unpaid judgment debt, settlement amount and costs in your proceedings against the trustees as proper defendant.
      `the value of the trust property` IS A NUMBER @desc The value of the trust property, in dollars.
GIVETH A NUMBER
`How much can I recover from the trustees` `the judgment, settlement and costs` `the value of the trust property` MEANS
    IF `the judgment, settlement and costs` AT MOST `the value of the trust property`
    THEN `the judgment, settlement and costs`
    ELSE `the value of the trust property`

§ `Part 4 -- An organisation's apology for child abuse (ss 7AA, 7AB)`

-- s 7AA(1): an apology by or on behalf of an organisation "in respect of child abuse allegedly perpetrated by an
-- individual associated with the organisation -- (a) does not constitute an express or implied admission of fault or
-- liability by the organisation ...; and (b) is not relevant to the determination of fault or liability ...; and (c) is
-- not admissible in any civil proceedings as evidence of the organisation's fault or liability". (3): "This section does
-- not apply to an apology in respect of child abuse that is given by or on behalf of an organisation, if the apology is
-- made by the alleged perpetrator of the abuse." (4): it applies even if the apology was given before the Justice
-- Miscellaneous (Commission of Inquiry) Act 2024 received the Royal Assent, the abuse was before the Act commenced, or
-- the apology does not meet subsection (2). s 7AB: the 2024 amendments "do not apply to civil proceedings that -- (a)
-- have been determined before that Act received the Royal Assent; or (b) have commenced, but have not been determined,
-- before that Act received the Royal Assent". s 6B: "apology means an expression of sympathy or regret, or of a general
-- sense of benevolence or compassion, which does not contain an admission of fault or liability".
@export Can the organisation's apology for the child abuse be used as evidence that it was at fault (Civil Liability Act 2002 (Tas) ss 6B, 7AA, 7AB)? No: an organisation's apology (sympathy or regret, not admitting fault) is not an admission, is not relevant to fault and is not admissible as evidence of it, whenever given and even if it does not follow s 7AA(2); but not if the apology was made by the alleged abuser, or in proceedings begun before the 2024 amending Act received the Royal Assent. Answers false where s 7AA applies, nothing otherwise.
GIVEN `the apology admits fault or liability` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the apology contain an admission of fault or liability (not just sympathy or regret)?
      `the apology was made by the alleged abuser` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the apology made by the person who allegedly abused you?
      `my proceedings began before the 2024 amending Act received Royal Assent` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Were your civil proceedings started (or decided) before the Justice Miscellaneous (Commission of Inquiry) Act 2024 received the Royal Assent?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can the organisation's apology be used as evidence of fault` `the apology admits fault or liability` `the apology was made by the alleged abuser` `my proceedings began before the 2024 amending Act received Royal Assent` MEANS
    IF          (NOT `the apology admits fault or liability`)
            AND (NOT `the apology was made by the alleged abuser`)
            AND (NOT `my proceedings began before the 2024 amending Act received Royal Assent`)
    THEN JUST FALSE
    ELSE NOTHING
